<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battambang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Battambang is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, on the Sangkae River in the north-west, and capital of Battambang Province. Ruled by Siam from 1795, the province was ceded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siem-reap">Siem Reap</a> to French-protected Cambodia by the Franco-Siamese treaty of 1907. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> reoccupied it from 1941 to 1946, and the surrounding plain is Cambodia's main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battambang.jpg" alt="Battambang" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maharaja1994 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Battambang_city.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Battambang lies on the Sangkae River, which winds through the province of the same name. The province is considered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> bowl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, and the city has many mills that process rice for national and international markets; its jasmine rice is well known. The river long served as the main link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a>, and the city is also joined to Phnom Penh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> by National Highway 5.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Battambang began as a fishing village on the Sangkae River in the 11th century, founded under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>. In 1795 Siam annexed much of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> as the province of Inner Cambodia and ruled Battambang as a provincial capital through the Abhaiwongse family. By 1880 it was an important trading city of about 2,500 residents. The Siamese built one road, a fort for the governor, a market, several pagodas and a Catholic hospital.</p><p>In 1907 the province was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and rejoined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>. The French laid out a grid of streets, canals and bridges in what is now the Heritage Conservation Area, linked both riverbanks by two bridges in 1917 and in 1926 began a second phase of building, with a railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>, villas, the central market and state buildings.</p><p>In 1953 Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norodom-sihanouk">Norodom Sihanouk</a> chose Battambang as the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>'s independence movement. It was Cambodia's second largest city in the 20th century, with garment factories, an airport, schools and a university. Under Democratic Kampuchea its population was forcibly evacuated into labour camps and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-tenure">land tenure</a> was abolished; the Battambang Provincial Museum became a prison, and the Phnom Sampeau killing caves, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> execution site, lie about six kilometres from the city. A Vietnamese invasion toppled the Khmer Rouge in 1980, but the province remained a Khmer Rouge stronghold into the 1990s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">Guerrillas</a> besieged the city in 1990 without taking it, and conflict around it continued until 1997.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Battambang is the capital of its province and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>'s larger cities. The municipality established a Heritage Protection Area in 2009 to safeguard around 800 historic buildings, among them French colonial architecture and the Art Deco central market, Psar Nath, built in 1936. Wat Damrei Sar, built in the 19th century, is the oldest pagoda in the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> named it a City of Gastronomy in 2023, the first Cambodian city in its Creative Cities Network.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battambang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jayavarman II's own links lay with Vyādhapura in the south, from which, Angkor-period inscriptions record, he took people to settle <b>Battambang</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chenla">Chenla · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>French treaties with Siam fixed the Mekong frontier and returned <b>Battambang</b>, Siem Reap and Sisophon to Cambodia in 1907, while the boundaries between the five territories became the international borders of three states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1907 France obtained <b>Battambang</b> and Siem Reap, with Angkor, from Siam.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-cambodia">French protectorate of Cambodia</a></li></ul></section>
